IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `ssc-core`

-- Social Security Contributions and Benefits Act 1992 (United Kingdom), 1992 c. 4.
-- SCHEDULE 7 (industrial injuries benefits), Part IV (reduced earnings allowance: paragraphs 11 and 12) and Part V (retirement
-- allowance: paragraph 13). The converter dropped the paragraph numbers. 11 is cited as "paragraph 11(10)" at src:5528 and src:5548;
-- Part IV's "Supplementary" is 12 and Part V's single paragraph is 13 by order of the headings (Part VI then begins at 14, which
-- 15(2) and 16 confirm by their citations). A "Schedule 4, Part V, paragraph N" amount is the omitted Schedule 4 figure, taken as an input.

§ `Schedule 7 Part IV -- reduced earnings allowance`

§§ `Paragraph 11(1) -- entitlement`

-- The exclusions are "to the extent that": a person is wholly, partly or not at all within each.
DECLARE `sch 7 pt IV Extent` IS ONE OF
    `sch 7 pt IV wholly`
    `sch 7 pt IV partly`
    `sch 7 pt IV not at all`

-- 11(1): an employed earner is entitled if (a) entitled to a disablement pension (or would be if it were payable where disablement is
-- assessed at not less than 1 per cent) and (b) as a result of the relevant loss of faculty either (i) incapable, and likely to remain
-- permanently incapable, of following the regular occupation and (ii) incapable of following employment of an equivalent standard which is
-- suitable, or has at all times since the end of the 90 days been incapable of following that occupation or any such employment. But not
-- to the extent that the loss of faculty results from an accident on or after 1 October 1990, and not for a disease prescribed on or after
-- 10 October 1994, nor (in so far as extended) for a disease prescribed earlier whose prescription is extended on or after that date.
-- src:5476 | (1) Subject to the provisions of this paragraph, an employed earner shall be entitled to reduced earnings allowance if—
-- src:5477 | (a) he is entitled to a disablement pension or would be so entitled if that pension were payable where disablement is assessed at not less than 1 per cent.; and
-- src:5478 | (b) as a result of the relevant loss of faculty, he is either—
-- src:5479 | (i) incapable, and likely to remain permanently incapable, of following his regular occupation; and
-- src:5480 | (ii) incapable of following employment of an equivalent standard which is suitable in his case,
-- src:5481 | or is, and has at all times since the end of the period of 90 days referred to in section 103(6) above been, incapable of following that occupation or any such employment;
-- src:5482 | but a person shall not be entitled to reduced earnings allowance to the extent that the relevant loss of faculty results from an accident happening on or after 1st October 1990 (the day on which section 3 of the Social Security Act 1990 came into force) and a person shall not be entitled to reduced earnings allowance—
-- src:5483 | (i) in relation to a disease prescribed on or after 10th October 1994 under section 108(2) above; or
-- src:5484 | (ii) in relation to a disease prescribed before 10th October 1994 whose prescription is extended on or after that date under section 108(2) above but only in so far as the prescription has been so extended.
@ref sch 7 pt IV para 11(1) (src:5476-5484)
GIVEN `the person is an employed earner` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person is entitled to a disablement pension, or would be if that pension were payable where disablement is assessed at not less than 1 per cent` IS A BOOLEAN
      `as a result of the relevant loss of faculty, the person is incapable, and likely to remain permanently incapable, of following the regular occupation` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person is incapable of following employment of an equivalent standard which is suitable in the person's case` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person is, and has at all times since the end of the period of 90 days been, incapable of following that occupation or any such employment` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the relevant loss of faculty results from an accident happening on or after 1 October 1990` IS A `sch 7 pt IV Extent`
      `the claim is in relation to a disease prescribed on or after 10 October 1994 under section 108(2)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the claim is in relation to a disease prescribed before 10 October 1994 whose prescription is extended on or after that date under section 108(2)` IS A `sch 7 pt IV Extent`
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`sch 7 pt IV para 11(1) the employed earner is entitled to reduced earnings allowance` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `the person is an employed earner`
            THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "an employed earner shall be entitled"
        IF NOT `the person is entitled to a disablement pension, or would be if that pension were payable where disablement is assessed at not less than 1 per cent`
            THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "(a) he is entitled to a disablement pension or would be so entitled"
        IF NOT ((`as a result of the relevant loss of faculty, the person is incapable, and likely to remain permanently incapable, of following the regular occupation` AND `the person is incapable of following employment of an equivalent standard which is suitable in the person's case`) OR `the person is, and has at all times since the end of the period of 90 days been, incapable of following that occupation or any such employment`)
            THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "(b) ... either (i) ... and (ii) ... or is, and has at all times since the end of the period of 90 days ... been, incapable"
        IF `the relevant loss of faculty results from an accident happening on or after 1 October 1990` EQUALS `sch 7 pt IV wholly`
            THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "a person shall not be entitled ... to the extent that the relevant loss of faculty results from an accident happening on or after 1st October 1990"
        IF `the claim is in relation to a disease prescribed on or after 10 October 1994 under section 108(2)`
            THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "a person shall not be entitled to reduced earnings allowance (i) in relation to a disease prescribed on or after 10th October 1994"
        IF `the claim is in relation to a disease prescribed before 10 October 1994 whose prescription is extended on or after that date under section 108(2)` EQUALS `sch 7 pt IV wholly`
            THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "(ii) in relation to a disease prescribed before 10th October 1994 whose prescription is extended on or after that date ... but only in so far as the prescription has been so extended"
        IF `the relevant loss of faculty results from an accident happening on or after 1 October 1990` EQUALS `sch 7 pt IV partly` OR `the claim is in relation to a disease prescribed before 10 October 1994 whose prescription is extended on or after that date under section 108(2)` EQUALS `sch 7 pt IV partly`
            THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "to the extent that" and "only in so far as" leave an entitlement for the part outside the exclusion, and the text does not apportion it
        OTHERWISE JUST TRUE

§§ `Paragraph 11(2) and (3) -- no second entitlement`

-- 11(2): a person who immediately before "that date" is entitled in consequence of the relevant accident, and subsequently ceases to be entitled
-- for one or more days, shall not again be entitled in consequence of that accident, but a claim or award is not prevented for a period that
-- commences not later than the day after the one on which the claimant was last entitled. "That date" has no antecedent in this sub-paragraph.
-- Two dates precede it in 11(1): 1 October 1990 (src:5482) and 10 October 1994 (src:5483-5484, where "that date" already means 10 October 1994).
-- 1 October 1990 is read, because 11(3)(a) (src:5490) speaks of entitlement "immediately before 1st October 1990" (NOTES.md, fork F8).
-- src:5485 | (2) A person—
-- src:5486 | (a) who immediately before that date is entitled to reduced earnings allowance in consequence of the relevant accident; but
-- src:5487 | (b) who subsequently ceases to be entitled to that allowance for one or more days,
-- src:5488 | shall not again be entitled to reduced earnings allowance in consequence of that accident; but this sub-paragraph does not prevent the making at any time of a claim for, or an award of, reduced earnings allowance in consequence of that accident for a period which commences not later than the day after that on which the claimant was last entitled to that allowance in consequence of that accident.
@ref sch 7 pt IV para 11(2) (src:5485-5488)
GIVEN `the person was entitled to reduced earnings allowance in consequence of the relevant accident immediately before 1 October 1990` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person subsequently ceased to be entitled to that allowance for one or more days` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day the claimant was last entitled to the allowance in consequence of that accident` IS A DATE
      `the day the period of the claim or award commences` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`sch 7 pt IV para 11(2) the person may again be entitled for the period` MEANS
    IF `the person was entitled to reduced earnings allowance in consequence of the relevant accident immediately before 1 October 1990` AND `the person subsequently ceased to be entitled to that allowance for one or more days`
    THEN
        IF `the day the period of the claim or award commences` AT MOST `the day the claimant was last entitled to the allowance in consequence of that accident` PLUS 1
        THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "this sub-paragraph does not prevent the making ... of a claim for, or an award of" says only that this bar does not apply; sub-paragraph (1) decides
        ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "shall not again be entitled to reduced earnings allowance in consequence of that accident"
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "A person (a) who ... but (b) who subsequently ceases"

-- 11(3)(a): a person who, apart from section 103(6), would have been entitled immediately before 1 October 1990 is treated as entitled on any
-- day (including a Sunday) on which he would have been entitled apart from that provision. (b) lets regulations prescribe other cases (inert, machinery).
-- src:5489 | (3) For the purposes of sub-paragraph (2) above—
-- src:5490 | (a) a person who, apart from section 103(6) above, would have been entitled to reduced earnings allowance immediately before 1st October 1990 shall be treated as entitled to that allowance on any day (including a Sunday) on which he would have been entitled to it apart from that provision;
@ref sch 7 pt IV para 11(3)(a) (src:5489-5490)
GIVEN `the person, apart from section 103(6), would have been entitled to reduced earnings allowance immediately before 1 October 1990` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person would have been entitled on the day, apart from section 103(6)` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`sch 7 pt IV para 11(3)(a) the person is treated as entitled on the day` MEANS
    IF `the person, apart from section 103(6), would have been entitled to reduced earnings allowance immediately before 1 October 1990` AND `the person would have been entitled on the day, apart from section 103(6)`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "a person who ... shall be treated as entitled ... on any day on which he would have been entitled" says nothing of anyone else

§§ `Paragraphs 11(5) and (6) -- regular occupation and equivalent standard`

-- 11(5)(a): references to a regular occupation are to be taken as not including any subsidiary occupation, except to the extent that regulations
-- under 11(4) treat it as included. 11(4) is a regulation-making power (inert, machinery).
-- src:5493 | (5) In sub-paragraph (1) above—
-- src:5494 | (a) references to a person’s regular occupation are to be taken as not including any subsidiary occupation, except to the extent that they fall to be treated as including such an occupation by virtue of regulations under sub-paragraph (4) above; and
@ref sch 7 pt IV para 11(5)(a) (src:5493-5494)
GIVEN `the occupation is a subsidiary occupation` IS A BOOLEAN
      `regulations under sub-paragraph (4) treat it as the regular occupation` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`sch 7 pt IV para 11(5)(a) the occupation counts as the regular occupation` MEANS
    IF `the occupation is a subsidiary occupation`
    THEN
        IF `regulations under sub-paragraph (4) treat it as the regular occupation`
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "references to a person's regular occupation are to be taken as not including any subsidiary occupation"
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the sub-paragraph only excludes subsidiary occupations; what is a regular occupation is not defined

-- 11(5)(b): employment of an equivalent standard "is to be taken as not including employment other than employed earner's employment".
-- src:5495 | (b) employment of an equivalent standard is to be taken as not including employment other than employed earner’s employment;
@ref sch 7 pt IV para 11(5)(b) (src:5495)
GIVEN `the employment is employed earner's employment` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`sch 7 pt IV para 11(5)(b) the employment may count as employment of an equivalent standard` MEANS
    IF `the employment is employed earner's employment`
    THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "equivalent standard" is otherwise undefined and turns on remuneration and prospects (the last clause of 11(5), which directs regard to be had to a reasonable prospect of advancement, is the next rule below)
    ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "is to be taken as not including employment other than employed earner's employment"

-- 11(5), last clause: "in assessing the standard of remuneration in any employment, including a person's regular occupation, regard is to be had to his
-- reasonable prospect of advancement." A direction on how the standard of remuneration is assessed: it holds whenever that assessment is being made.
-- src:5496 | and in assessing the standard of remuneration in any employment, including a person’s regular occupation, regard is to be had to his reasonable prospect of advancement.
@ref sch 7 pt IV para 11(5) (src:5496)
GIVEN `the standard of remuneration in an employment, including the person's regular occupation, is being assessed` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`sch 7 pt IV para 11(5) regard is to be had to the reasonable prospect of advancement` MEANS
    IF `the standard of remuneration in an employment, including the person's regular occupation, is being assessed`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "in assessing the standard of remuneration in any employment": the direction speaks only of that assessment

-- 11(6) (last limb): so long as the person is, as a result of the relevant loss of faculty, deprived in whole or in part of the normal prospects of
-- advancement in the occupation, the person "is to be treated as incapable of following that occupation".
-- src:5497 | (6) For the purposes of this Part of this Schedule a person’s regular occupation is to be treated as extending to and including employment in the capacities to which the persons in that occupation (or a class or description of them to which he belonged at the time of the relevant accident) are in the normal course advanced, and to which, if he had continued to follow that occupation without having suffered the relevant loss of faculty, he would have had at least the normal prospects of advancement; and so long as he is, as a result of the relevant loss of faculty, deprived in whole or in part of those prospects, he is to be treated as incapable of following that occupation.
@ref sch 7 pt IV para 11(6) (src:5497)
GIVEN `as a result of the relevant loss of faculty, the person is deprived in whole or in part of the prospects of advancement that the person would have had at least normally` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`sch 7 pt IV para 11(6) the person is treated as incapable of following the regular occupation` MEANS
    IF `as a result of the relevant loss of faculty, the person is deprived in whole or in part of the prospects of advancement that the person would have had at least normally`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "so long as he is, as a result of the relevant loss of faculty, deprived in whole or in part of those prospects, he is to be treated as incapable" (a failed "so long as")

§§ `Paragraph 11(8) to (12A) -- period, rate and limits`

-- 11(8)(b): if at the end of the period fixed at the award the beneficiary submits a fresh claim, the allowance is awarded "for such further
-- period, commencing as mentioned in sub-paragraph (2)", as may be determined. (8)(a): "for such period as may be determined" is the award itself.
-- src:5499 | (8) Reduced earnings allowance shall be awarded—
-- src:5500 | (a) for such period as may be determined at the time of the award; and
-- src:5501 | (b) if at the end of that period the beneficiary submits a fresh claim for the allowance, for such further period, commencing as mentioned in sub-paragraph (2) above, as may be determined.
@ref sch 7 pt IV para 11(8) (src:5499-5501)
GIVEN `at the end of the period fixed at the award the beneficiary submits a fresh claim for the allowance` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`sch 7 pt IV para 11(8)(b) the allowance may be awarded for a further period` MEANS
    IF `at the end of the period fixed at the award the beneficiary submits a fresh claim for the allowance`
    THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): "for such further period ... as may be determined"
    ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "if at the end of that period the beneficiary submits a fresh claim for the allowance" (a failed "if")

-- 11(9): the award "may not be for a period longer than the period to be taken into account under paragraph 4 or 6 of Schedule 6". Read as a limit
-- on LENGTH (marginal reading (a)); the other reading (b) limits where the award may END, and the two agree whenever the award starts with the assessed period.
-- src:5502 | (9) The award may not be for a period longer than the period to be taken into account under paragraph 4 or 6 of Schedule 6 to this Act.
@ref sch 7 pt IV para 11(9) (src:5502)
GIVEN `the period to be taken into account under paragraph 4 or 6 of Schedule 6` IS A Period
      `the period of the award` IS A Period
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`sch 7 pt IV para 11(9) the award may be for the period` MEANS
    IF length award GREATER THAN length assessed
    THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "may not be for a period longer than the period to be taken into account under paragraph 4 or 6 of Schedule 6"
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "may not be longer" is a limit and no grant
    WHERE
        length p MEANS (Day (p's `the last day`)) MINUS (Day (p's `the first day`)) PLUS 1
        award MEANS `the period of the award`
        assessed MEANS `the period to be taken into account under paragraph 4 or 6 of Schedule 6`

-- 11(14) (and 13(11), the same): "maximum rate of a disablement pension" is the rate in the first entry in column (2) of Schedule 4 Part V
-- paragraph 1, without increases of disablement pension: the Schedule 4 amount, taken as an input.
-- src:5514 | (14) In this paragraph “maximum rate of a disablement pension” means the rate specified in the first entry in column (2) of Schedule 4, Part V, paragraph 1 and does not include increases in disablement pension under any provision of this Act.
@ref sch 7 pt IV para 11(14) (src:5514)
GIVEN `sch 4 pt V para 1 the rate in the first entry in column (2), in pounds` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`sch 7 pt IV para 11(14) the maximum rate of a disablement pension` MEANS
    `sch 4 pt V para 1 the rate in the first entry in column (2), in pounds`

-- 11(12A): the reference in 11(11) to a person who has retired from regular employment includes a person treated under s 27(3) of the 1975 Act as
-- having retired and a person deemed under s 27(5) to have retired.
-- src:5508 | (12A) The reference in sub-paragraph (11) above to a person who has retired from regular employment includes a reference—
-- src:5509 | (a) to a person who under subsection (3) of section 27 of the 1975 Act was treated for the purposes of that Act as having retired from regular employment; and
-- src:5510 | (b) to a person who under subsection (5) of that section was deemed for those purposes to have retired from it.
@ref sch 7 pt IV para 11(12A) (src:5508-5510)
GIVEN `the person has retired from regular employment` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person was treated under subsection (3) of section 27 of the 1975 Act as having retired from regular employment` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person was deemed under subsection (5) of that section to have retired from regular employment` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`sch 7 pt IV para 11(12A) the person is a person who retired from regular employment` MEANS
    `the person has retired from regular employment` OR `the person was treated under subsection (3) of section 27 of the 1975 Act as having retired from regular employment` OR `the person was deemed under subsection (5) of that section to have retired from regular employment`

-- 11(10) and (11): the rate is determined by reference to the probable standard of remuneration (a judgment, not a formula), "but in no case at a
-- rate higher than 40 per cent. of the maximum rate of a disablement pension or at a rate such that the aggregate of disablement pension (not
-- including increases) and reduced earnings allowance ... exceeds 140 per cent." of that maximum rate; for a person who retired from regular
-- employment before 6 April 1987 "100 per cent." replaces "140 per cent.". The highest rate is the lesser of the two caps, and not below nought.
-- src:5503 | (10) Reduced earnings allowance shall be payable at a rate determined by reference to the beneficiary’s probable standard of remuneration during the period for which it is granted in any employed earner’s employments which are suitable in his case and which he is likely to be capable of following as compared with that in the relevant occupation, but in no case at a rate higher than 40 per cent. of the maximum rate of a disablement pension or at a rate such that the aggregate of disablement pension (not including increases in disablement pension under any provision of this Act) and reduced earnings allowance awarded to the beneficiary exceeds 140 per cent. of the maximum rate of a disablement pension.
-- src:5504 | (11) Sub-paragraph (10) above shall have effect in the case of a person who retired from regular employment before 6th April 1987 with the substitution for “140 per cent." of “ 100 per cent. ”.
@ref sch 7 pt IV para 11(10) and (11) (src:5503-5504)
GIVEN `the maximum rate of a disablement pension` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
      `the weekly rate of the disablement pension, not including increases` IS A NUMBER
      `the person retired from regular employment before 6 April 1987` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`sch 7 pt IV para 11(10) the highest rate of reduced earnings allowance` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the maximum rate of a disablement pension`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the Schedule 4 table is omitted from this text
    WHEN JUST maximum THEN JUST (max 0 (min (maximum TIMES 0.4) ((maximum TIMES aggregate) MINUS `the weekly rate of the disablement pension, not including increases`)))
    WHERE
        aggregate MEANS IF `the person retired from regular employment before 6 April 1987` THEN 1 ELSE 1.4

-- src:5503 | (10) Reduced earnings allowance shall be payable at a rate determined by reference to the beneficiary’s probable standard of remuneration during the period for which it is granted in any employed earner’s employments which are suitable in his case and which he is likely to be capable of following as compared with that in the relevant occupation, but in no case at a rate higher than 40 per cent. of the maximum rate of a disablement pension or at a rate such that the aggregate of disablement pension (not including increases in disablement pension under any provision of this Act) and reduced earnings allowance awarded to the beneficiary exceeds 140 per cent. of the maximum rate of a disablement pension.
@ref sch 7 pt IV para 11(10) (src:5503)
GIVEN `the rate determined by reference to the probable standard of remuneration` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
      `the highest rate of reduced earnings allowance` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`sch 7 pt IV para 11(10) the rate of reduced earnings allowance` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the rate determined by reference to the probable standard of remuneration`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (undefined term): "a rate determined by reference to the beneficiary's probable standard of remuneration" is a judgment, and on any award but the first it is for regulations (11(13), inert)
    WHEN JUST wanted THEN
        CONSIDER `the highest rate of reduced earnings allowance`
        WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING
        WHEN JUST cap THEN JUST (min wanted cap)

-- 11(12): "the relevant occupation" is, for a person entitled by virtue of regulations under 11(4), the occupation in which the person was engaged
-- when the relevant accident took place; for any other person it is the regular occupation within the meaning of 11(1).
-- src:5505 | (12) In sub-paragraph (10) above “the relevant occupation” means—
-- src:5506 | (a) in relation to a person who is entitled to reduced earnings allowance by virtue of regulations under sub-paragraph (4) above, the occupation in which he was engaged when the relevant accident took place; and
-- src:5507 | (b) in relation to any other person who is entitled to reduced earnings allowance, his regular occupation within the meaning of sub-paragraph (1) above.
@ref sch 7 pt IV para 11(12) (src:5505-5507)
GIVEN `the person is entitled by virtue of regulations under sub-paragraph (4)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the occupation in which the person was engaged when the relevant accident took place` IS A STRING
      `the person's regular occupation within the meaning of sub-paragraph (1)` IS A STRING
GIVETH A STRING
`sch 7 pt IV para 11(12) the relevant occupation` MEANS
    IF `the person is entitled by virtue of regulations under sub-paragraph (4)`
    THEN `the occupation in which the person was engaged when the relevant accident took place`
    ELSE `the person's regular occupation within the meaning of sub-paragraph (1)`

§§ `Paragraph 12 -- supplementary: reduced earnings allowance for life`

-- 12(1): a person who on 10 April 1988 or 9 April 1989 (a) has attained pensionable age, (b) has retired from regular employment and (c) is entitled
-- to reduced earnings allowance "shall be entitled to that allowance for life". "A person who" describes the person the sentence is about.
-- src:5516 | (1) A person who on 10th April 1988 or 9th April 1989 satisfies the conditions—
-- src:5517 | (a) that he has attained pensionable age;
-- src:5518 | (b) that he has retired from regular employment; and
-- src:5519 | (c) that he is entitled to reduced earnings allowance,
-- src:5520 | shall be entitled to that allowance for life.
@ref sch 7 pt IV para 12(1) (src:5516-5520)
GIVEN day IS A DATE
      `the person has attained pensionable age on the day` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person has retired from regular employment on the day` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person is entitled to reduced earnings allowance on the day` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`sch 7 pt IV para 12(1) the person is entitled to reduced earnings allowance for life` MEANS
    IF (day EQUALS Date 10 4 1988 OR day EQUALS Date 9 4 1989) AND `the person has attained pensionable age on the day` AND `the person has retired from regular employment on the day` AND `the person is entitled to reduced earnings allowance on the day`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "A person who on 10th April 1988 or 9th April 1989 satisfies the conditions" says nothing of anyone else

-- 12(5) and (6): the "relevant date" is 10 April 1988 for a beneficiary entitled by satisfying the conditions on that date, and 9 April 1989 for
-- one entitled "by virtue only of" satisfying them on 9 April 1989.
-- src:5529 | (5) For a beneficiary who is entitled to reduced earnings allowance by virtue of satisfying the conditions in sub-paragraph (1) above on 10th April 1988 the relevant date is that date.
-- src:5530 | (6) For a beneficiary who is entitled to it by virtue only of satisfying those conditions on 9th April 1989 the relevant date is that date.
@ref sch 7 pt IV para 12(5) and (6) (src:5529-5530)
GIVEN `the beneficiary satisfies the conditions in sub-paragraph (1) on 10 April 1988` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the beneficiary satisfies the conditions in sub-paragraph (1) on 9 April 1989` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`sch 7 pt IV para 12(5) the relevant date` MEANS
    IF `the beneficiary satisfies the conditions in sub-paragraph (1) on 10 April 1988`
    THEN JUST (Date 10 4 1988)
    ELSE IF `the beneficiary satisfies the conditions in sub-paragraph (1) on 9 April 1989`
         THEN JUST (Date 9 4 1989)
         ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the relevant date is defined for a beneficiary entitled by virtue of sub-paragraph (1)

-- 12(4): a "qualifying beneficiary" is a person entitled by virtue of 12(1) who (a) did not attain pensionable age before 6 April 1987 or
-- (b) did not retire from regular employment before that date, and who on the relevant date was entitled to the allowance at a rate which was
-- restricted under paragraph 11(10) by reference to 40 per cent. of the maximum rate of disablement pension.
-- src:5525 | (4) In sub-paragraph (3) above “qualifying beneficiary” means a person entitled to reduced earnings allowance by virtue of sub-paragraph (1) above who—
-- src:5526 | (a) did not attain pensionable age before 6th April 1987, or
-- src:5527 | (b) did not retire from regular employment before that date,
-- src:5528 | and who, on the relevant date, was entitled to the allowance at a rate which was restricted under paragraph 11(10) above by reference to 40 per cent. of the maximum rate of disablement pension.
@ref sch 7 pt IV para 12(4) (src:5525-5528)
GIVEN `the person is entitled to reduced earnings allowance by virtue of sub-paragraph (1)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person did not attain pensionable age before 6 April 1987` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person did not retire from regular employment before 6 April 1987` IS A BOOLEAN
      `on the relevant date the person was entitled to the allowance at a rate restricted under paragraph 11(10) by reference to 40 per cent. of the maximum rate of disablement pension` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`sch 7 pt IV para 12(4) the person is a qualifying beneficiary` MEANS
    `the person is entitled to reduced earnings allowance by virtue of sub-paragraph (1)` AND (`the person did not attain pensionable age before 6 April 1987` OR `the person did not retire from regular employment before 6 April 1987`) AND `on the relevant date the person was entitled to the allowance at a rate restricted under paragraph 11(10) by reference to 40 per cent. of the maximum rate of disablement pension`

-- 12(2) and (3): the allowance is payable at the weekly rate at which it was payable on the relevant date (or would have been but for any
-- enactment or regulations); for a qualifying beneficiary it is assumed that rate was £25.84 where that date is 10 April 1988 and £26.96 where it is 9 April 1989.
-- src:5521 | (2) In the case of any beneficiary who is entitled to reduced earnings allowance by virtue of sub-paragraph (1) above, the allowance shall be payable, subject to any enactment contained in Part V or VI of this Act or in the Administration Act and to any regulations made under any such enactment, at the weekly rate at which it was payable to the beneficiary on the relevant date or would have been payable to him on that date but for any such enactment or regulations.
-- src:5522 | (3) For the purpose of determining under sub-paragraph (2) above the weekly rate of reduced earnings allowance payable in the case of a qualifying beneficiary, it shall be assumed that the weekly rate at which the allowance was payable to him on the relevant date was—
-- src:5523 | (a) £25.84, where that date is 10th April 1988, or
-- src:5524 | (b) £26.96, where that date is 9th April 1989.
@ref sch 7 pt IV para 12(2) and (3) (src:5521-5524)
GIVEN `the person is a qualifying beneficiary` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the relevant date` IS A DATE
      `the weekly rate at which the allowance was payable on the relevant date, or would have been but for any enactment or regulations` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`sch 7 pt IV para 12(2) the weekly rate of the allowance for life` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `the person is a qualifying beneficiary`
            THEN `the weekly rate at which the allowance was payable on the relevant date, or would have been but for any enactment or regulations`
        IF `the relevant date` EQUALS Date 10 4 1988
            THEN JUST 25.84
        IF `the relevant date` EQUALS Date 9 4 1989
            THEN JUST 26.96
        OTHERWISE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): the assumption is given for those two dates only

-- 12(7): the reference in 12(1) to a person who has retired from regular employment includes the two persons of 11(12A).
-- src:5531 | (7) The reference in sub-paragraph (1) above to a person who has retired from regular employment includes a reference—
-- src:5532 | (a) to a person who under subsection (3) of section 27 of the 1975 Act was treated for the purposes of that Act as having retired from regular employment; and
-- src:5533 | (b) to a person who under subsection (5) of that section was deemed for those purposes to have retired from it.
@ref sch 7 pt IV para 12(7) (src:5531-5533)
GIVEN `the person has retired from regular employment` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person was treated under subsection (3) of section 27 of the 1975 Act as having retired from regular employment` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the person was deemed under subsection (5) of that section to have retired from regular employment` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`sch 7 pt IV para 12(7) the person is a person who has retired from regular employment` MEANS
    `sch 7 pt IV para 11(12A) the person is a person who retired from regular employment` `the person has retired from regular employment` `the person was treated under subsection (3) of section 27 of the 1975 Act as having retired from regular employment` `the person was deemed under subsection (5) of that section to have retired from regular employment`

§ `Schedule 7 Part V -- retirement allowance`

§§ `Paragraph 13 -- retirement allowance`

-- 13(1): a person who (a) has attained pensionable age, (b) gives up regular employment on or after 10 April 1989 and (c) was entitled to reduced earnings
-- allowance on the day immediately before, "shall cease to be entitled to reduced earnings allowance as from the day on which he gives up regular employment".
-- src:5535 | (1) Subject to the provisions of this Part of this Schedule, a person who—
-- src:5536 | (a) has attained pensionable age; and
-- src:5537 | (b) gives up regular employment on or after 10th April 1989; and
-- src:5538 | (c) was entitled to reduced earnings allowance (by virtue either of one award or of a number of awards) on the day immediately before he gave up such employment,
-- src:5539 | shall cease to be entitled to reduced earnings allowance as from the day on which he gives up regular employment.
@ref sch 7 pt V para 13(1) (src:5535-5539)
GIVEN `the person has attained pensionable age` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day the person gives up regular employment` IS A DATE
      `the person was entitled to reduced earnings allowance on the day immediately before` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`sch 7 pt V para 13(1) the day from which the person ceases to be entitled to reduced earnings allowance` MEANS
    IF `the person has attained pensionable age` AND (`the day the person gives up regular employment` AT LEAST Date 10 4 1989) AND `the person was entitled to reduced earnings allowance on the day immediately before`
    THEN JUST `the day the person gives up regular employment`
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "a person who ... shall cease to be entitled" says nothing of anyone else

-- 13(2): if on the day before ceasing under 13(1) the person is entitled to reduced earnings allowance at a weekly rate (or aggregate weekly rate,
-- over one or more awards) of not less than £2.00, the person "shall be entitled to a benefit, to be known as 'retirement allowance'".
-- src:5540 | (2) If the day before a person ceases under sub-paragraph (1) above to be entitled to reduced earnings allowance he is entitled to the allowance (by virtue either of one award or of a number of awards) at a weekly rate or aggregate weekly rate of not less than £2.00, he shall be entitled to a benefit, to be known as “retirement allowance”.
@ref sch 7 pt V para 13(2) (src:5540)
GIVEN `the person ceases to be entitled to reduced earnings allowance under sub-paragraph (1)` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the weekly rate, or aggregate weekly rate, at which the person is entitled to the allowance on the day before` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`sch 7 pt V para 13(2) the person is entitled to retirement allowance` MEANS
    IF `the person ceases to be entitled to reduced earnings allowance under sub-paragraph (1)`
    THEN
        IF `the weekly rate, or aggregate weekly rate, at which the person is entitled to the allowance on the day before` AT LEAST 2
        THEN JUST TRUE
        ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "If the day before ... he is entitled ... at a weekly rate ... of not less than £2.00, he shall be entitled" (a failed "if")
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): sub-paragraph (2) speaks of a person who "ceases under sub-paragraph (1) above"

-- src:5540 | (2) If the day before a person ceases under sub-paragraph (1) above to be entitled to reduced earnings allowance he is entitled to the allowance (by virtue either of one award or of a number of awards) at a weekly rate or aggregate weekly rate of not less than £2.00, he shall be entitled to a benefit, to be known as “retirement allowance”.
@ref sch 7 pt V para 13(2) (src:5540)
GIVETH A Benefit
`sch 7 pt V para 13(2) what the benefit is called` MEANS `retirement allowance`

-- 13(3): retirement allowance "shall be payable ... for life" (subject to other enactments and regulations).
-- src:5541 | (3) Retirement allowance shall be payable to him (subject to any enactment contained in Part V or VI of this Act or in the Administration Act and to any regulations made under any such enactment) for life.
@ref sch 7 pt V para 13(3) (src:5541)
GIVEN `the person is alive on the day` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`sch 7 pt V para 13(3) retirement allowance may be payable on the day` MEANS
    IF `the person is alive on the day`
    THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "subject to any enactment contained in Part V or VI of this Act or in the Administration Act and to any regulations made under any such enactment"
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "payable ... for life" is a stated period; the sub-paragraph does not say the allowance is unpayable after death

-- 13(5): for a beneficiary who retires or is deemed to have retired on 10 April 1989 and who on 9 April 1989 was entitled at a rate restricted under
-- 11(10) by reference to 40 per cent. of the maximum rate, it is assumed that the weekly rate of reduced earnings allowance on 9 April 1989 was £26.96.
-- src:5546 | (5) For the purpose of determining under sub-paragraph (4) above the weekly rate of retirement allowance in the case of a beneficiary who—
-- src:5547 | (a) retires or is deemed to have retired on 10th April 1989, and
-- src:5548 | (b) on 9th April 1989 was entitled to reduced earnings allowance at a rate which was restricted under paragraph 11(10) above by reference to 40 per cent. of the maximum rate of disablement pension,
-- src:5549 | it shall be assumed that the weekly rate of reduced earnings allowance to which he was entitled on 9th April 1989 was £26.96.
@ref sch 7 pt V para 13(5) (src:5546-5549)
GIVEN `the beneficiary retires or is deemed to have retired on 10 April 1989` IS A BOOLEAN
      `on 9 April 1989 the beneficiary was entitled to reduced earnings allowance at a rate restricted under paragraph 11(10) by reference to 40 per cent. of the maximum rate of disablement pension` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the weekly rate at which the beneficiary was last entitled to reduced earnings allowance` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`sch 7 pt V para 13(5) the weekly rate of reduced earnings allowance taken for sub-paragraph (4)` MEANS
    IF `the beneficiary retires or is deemed to have retired on 10 April 1989` AND `on 9 April 1989 the beneficiary was entitled to reduced earnings allowance at a rate restricted under paragraph 11(10) by reference to 40 per cent. of the maximum rate of disablement pension`
    THEN 26.96
    ELSE `the weekly rate at which the beneficiary was last entitled to reduced earnings allowance`

-- 13(4): the weekly rate is the lesser of (a) 25 per cent. of the weekly rate at which the beneficiary was last entitled to reduced earnings allowance and
-- (b) 10 per cent. of the maximum rate of a disablement pension, subject to 13(6).
-- src:5542 | (4) Subject to sub-paragraph (6) below, the weekly rate of a beneficiary’s retirement allowance shall be—
-- src:5543 | (a) 25 per cent. of the weekly rate at which he was last entitled to reduced earnings allowance; or
-- src:5544 | (b) 10 per cent. of the maximum rate of a disablement pension,
-- src:5545 | whichever is the less.
@ref sch 7 pt V para 13(4) (src:5542-5545)
GIVEN `the weekly rate at which the beneficiary was last entitled to reduced earnings allowance` IS A NUMBER
      `the maximum rate of a disablement pension` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`sch 7 pt V para 13(4) the weekly rate of retirement allowance before rounding` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the maximum rate of a disablement pension`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the Schedule 4 table is omitted from this text
    WHEN JUST maximum THEN JUST (min (`the weekly rate at which the beneficiary was last entitled to reduced earnings allowance` TIMES 0.25) (maximum TIMES 0.1))

-- 13(6): if the weekly rate (a) would not be a whole number of pence and (b) would exceed the whole number of pence next below it by 1/2p or more, the
-- beneficiary is entitled at the next higher whole number of pence. The sub-paragraph says nothing of a fraction under 1/2p (marginal reading, NOTES.md F9).
-- src:5550 | (6) If the weekly rate of the beneficiary’s retirement allowance—
-- src:5551 | (a) would not be a whole number of pence; and
-- src:5552 | (b) would exceed the whole number of pence next below it by 1/2p or more,
-- src:5553 | the beneficiary shall be entitled to retirement allowance at a rate equal to the next higher whole number of pence.
@ref sch 7 pt V para 13(6) (src:5550-5553)
GIVEN `the weekly rate of retirement allowance before rounding, in pounds` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`sch 7 pt V para 13(6) the weekly rate of retirement allowance after rounding` MEANS
    IF fraction EQUALS 0
    THEN JUST `the weekly rate of retirement allowance before rounding, in pounds`
    ELSE IF fraction AT LEAST 0.5
         THEN JUST ((below PLUS 1) DIVIDED BY 100)
         ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "would exceed the whole number of pence next below it by 1/2p or more"; the text does not round a fraction of less than 1/2p
    WHERE
        pence MEANS `the weekly rate of retirement allowance before rounding, in pounds` TIMES 100
        below MEANS FLOOR pence
        fraction MEANS pence MINUS below

-- 13(10): "Day of interruption of employment" means a day which forms part of (a) a jobseeking period or (b) a linked period, as defined by the Jobseekers Act 1995
-- (not among the sources, so each answer is an input).
-- src:5565 | (10) “Day of interruption of employment” means a day which forms part of—
-- src:5566 | (a) a jobseeking period (as defined by the Jobseekers Act 1995), or
-- src:5567 | (b) a linked period (as defined by that Act).
@ref sch 7 pt V para 13(10) (src:5565-5567)
GIVEN `the day forms part of a jobseeking period (as defined by the Jobseekers Act 1995)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the day forms part of a linked period (as defined by that Act)` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`sch 7 pt V para 13(10) the day is a day of interruption of employment` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `the day forms part of a jobseeking period (as defined by the Jobseekers Act 1995)` EQUALS JUST TRUE OR `the day forms part of a linked period (as defined by that Act)` EQUALS JUST TRUE
            THEN JUST TRUE
        IF `the day forms part of a jobseeking period (as defined by the Jobseekers Act 1995)` EQUALS JUST FALSE AND `the day forms part of a linked period (as defined by that Act)` EQUALS JUST FALSE
            THEN JUST FALSE
        OTHERWISE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the Jobseekers Act 1995 answer is missing

-- 13(11): "maximum rate of a disablement pension" in this paragraph is the same figure as 11(14).
-- src:5568 | (11) In this paragraph “maximum rate of a disablement pension” means the rate specified in the first entry in column (2) of Schedule 4, Part V, paragraph 1 and does not include increases in disablement pension under any provision of this Act.
@ref sch 7 pt V para 13(11) (src:5568)
GIVEN `sch 4 pt V para 1 the rate in the first entry in column (2), in pounds` IS A MAYBE NUMBER
GIVETH A MAYBE NUMBER
`sch 7 pt V para 13(11) the maximum rate of a disablement pension` MEANS
    `sch 7 pt IV para 11(14) the maximum rate of a disablement pension` `sch 4 pt V para 1 the rate in the first entry in column (2), in pounds`
